WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:06.000
you know when you're going through
airport security and you've

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.920
been good you've taken off all your
jewelry put your phone in the bucket

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.567
belt off too and yet when

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:13.280
you go through the scanner

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:19.680
the alarm still goes off
when this happens we we call this a

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.960
false positive
it's when the alarm goes off for no

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.720
apparent reason
and ends up when you get in the uh

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.160
but then while this is all happening you
you suddenly remember you left like a

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.880
tube of toothpaste or something in your
hand luggage

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.719
and yet that manages to go through the
scanner no problem

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:42.840
when this happens we call the outcome a
false

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.360
negative
so in other words false positives are

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.520
negative so when the
outcomes of a a test or experiment

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.719
doesn't actually reflect what happened
in reality

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:58.160
and these errors can apply to all kinds
of things from

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.840
security systems to things like forensic
evidence and

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.760
even cancer screenings and so they're
really important for us to understand

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.720
because well...
they can affect our personal lives

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.799
especially with this whole COVID
situation as i'll explain shortly

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:16.560
but before i go any further, first we
have to deal with

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.642
this abomination

00:01:17.642 --> 00:01:21.360
now you may have heard
these delightfully clear and not

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.119
remotely confusing terms being used in
medicine before

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:29.759
sensitivity is how often a test gives a
positive result for all actual cases

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:35.119
which means it's equal to
one minus the false negative rate

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:39.360
and specificity is how often a test
gives a negative result for all

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.920
non-cases, so it's equal to one minus the
false positive rate

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.280
but if you're anything like me then
honestly this is about as memorable as

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.280
someone's name you just got told at a
conference

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.880
so here's a handier way of remembering
them

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.920
a mouse's existence mostly seems to be
this sort of abject

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.479
misery of stress, constantly
freaking out at anything that moves

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:06.159
which to be fair is pretty justified
seeing as how small and vulnerable they

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.319
are and so they have to make sure they catch

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:10.879
every real threat in order to just
survive

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:15.440
and so to put that into medical terms
that means they are going for 100%

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.160
sensitivity to danger because they
simply can't handle any false negatives

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.560
then at the other end of the spectrum we
have this guy.  Being a lion means that

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.760
you lording over everyone,
and when you're at the top of the food

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.800
chain you don't have to worry about
getting eaten much and so

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.160
lions tend to hate being bothered about
anything unless it's absolutely necessary

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:39.040
and so to put that into medical speak

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.959
it means that lions are a hundred
percent specific

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:46.480
because they can't stand for any
false positives that make them

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.080
have to move.
so in an ideal world we want to ensure

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.040
that whatever test we're using to
measure something

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.319
has both a low false positive and
low false negative rate so that it's

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:04.239
maximally accurate...
but in reality that can be very hard to

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:07.920
do as often there's a direct trade-off
between these two things like

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.840
it's hard for a mouse to simultaneously
be alert for danger at all times

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.599
but also hang out in the sun like a lion
all day

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:19.519
so depending on the situation we
typically prefer to maximize

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:22.800
one over the other depending on which
outcome

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.920
is worse like with something like an
airport scanner

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.959
even though terrorists are fortunately
very rare

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:32.000
a false negative is still really really
bad because, well, 911

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.400
and the same is largely true
for disease diagnosis

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.959
while obviously doctors would prefer to
have a perfectly accurate track record

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:44.560
they'd still rather tests give the
odd false positive and needlessly worry

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.840
someone than
let someone who really does have cancer

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.200
slip through the net and then not get
the urgent treatment that they need

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.640
however there are some situations where
having a high

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.159
false positive rate can be just as
dangerous

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:01.280
and unfortunately COVID 19 might be
one of them

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.720
to understand why let's imagine we have
a thousand people who want to test for

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.200
COVID
and at the time of testing we predict

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.959
that say roughly two percent
of the general population has actually

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:13.760
had the virus
and let's say the test we're going to

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.600
use in this case an antibody test has
these error rates

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.359
which by the way are the accuracy of
some of the tests in real life

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:25.120
so given all this information what do
you think the likelihood is that

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.479
if you end up testing positive that you
actually

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:32.960
had COVID in this instance? Before you
try and calculate it take a moment to

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:37.040
just feel out whatever number first pops
into your head

00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:40.479
if you're anything like me the first
thing that came to mind was something

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:43.280
like a little under 94% right?

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.280
Surely whatever it is it's got to be
more likely that you've had the disease

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.800
then you've NOT had it, considering
you've had a positive test result...

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.720
well bizarrely that's 
completely not the case

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.840
despite the positive test result we are
still

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.199
most likely to have not had the virus in
this instance, and here's why:

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:06.160
so because of the 2%
estimated background rate at the time of

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.840
testing, then out of those 1000 people,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.039
on average only 20 will have actually had the COVs,

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:18.639
and that means 980 haven't.
then because of the test's 95% sensitivity

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:23.600
and 5%
false negative rate, 19 of those 20 cases

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.840
correctly test positive and one
unfortunate soul gets a false

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:35.199
negative, meanwhile out of the 980 who
haven't had the virus 94% of them

00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:38.880
get the true negative result but
crucially

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.320
the other six percent get a false
positive result

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.120
and that equals 59 people.
so if we then compare these two

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.440
categories of positive test results
we can see that the false ones outweigh

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.167
the true ones by around three to one.

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:55.199
in other words

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:57.840
despite your positive test results you
are still

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.759
way more likely to have never had the
virus... and to be abundantly clear this

00:06:01.759 --> 00:06:04.080
relates only to this specific
hypothetical example.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.800
And this counterintuitive phenomenon
is known as the false positive paradox

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.319
it's a thing that happens when a disease
is still

00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:16.960
rare enough that the base rate which
is the name for the fraction of the

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.919
general population who's actually had
the disease

00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:23.919
is smaller than the false positive rate
of the test

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:27.600
And this messes with our heads
because we tend to focus on the

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.800
high accuracy of the test
and forget about this crucial first step

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.400
of taking the base rate
into account. And this common error

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.080
has a name too:
we call it base rate neglect and it's a

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.600
consistent problem for both scientists
and public alike

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:47.600
so just think about what this means for
COVID testing if the background rate

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.560
of infection
is still low enough right now to be

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.599
similar to the false positive rate of
whatever test

00:06:53.599 --> 00:06:58.240
we're using then the majority of people
who do test positive

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:04.240
don't actually have immunity so
while widespread testing is crucial

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.800
individually, we still 
need to tread very carefully

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.599
with any results we get until we
confidently know

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:13.199
what the true background rate of
infection currently is

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:16.240
it's also really important to remember
that base rate neglect and the false

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.759
positive paradox aren't just dangerous
for things like health care,

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:24.000
they can screw up lives in other ways
too, and in fact the most egregious

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.080
example of this
is the case of Sally clark.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.960
Sally was a seemingly ordinary
law-abiding woman but who found herself

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.360
at the center of a national murder case
after two of her infant children died

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.680
when they reached a few months old.
Her defense argued that they had both

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.000
died of what is known as
sudden infant death syndrome, a rare

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.520
but awful natural phenomenon, but the
prosecutors argued that this disease was

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.639
so rare that the odds of both of these
children

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:58.000
independently dying of it was as slim as
1 in 73 million

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.960
odds so tiny, that
despite there being no other evidence

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.840
against her it was sufficient enough to
convince

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.479
judge and jury to imprison sally for
life.

00:08:10.479 --> 00:08:14.400
But in reality there were so many
outrageously basic errors in their

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.680
analysis that they almost certainly
sent an innocent woman

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.160
to jail:
firstly, that calculation of 8 500

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:27.360
squared to get to 1 in 73 million
makes the huge assumption that the two

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.120
deaths were completely
 independent events, but this

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.320
whole assumption is not remotely based
in reality

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:37.760
because it's now known that SIDS can run
in families due to both

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:42.080
environmental and genetic risk factors&gt;
That alone would have reduced the

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.279
prosecution's odds argument drastically.
But the prosecutor's

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:49.120
real statistical crime was

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.959
base rate neglect,
because while double SIDS deaths are

00:08:52.959 --> 00:08:57.440
extremely rare,
guess what are even rarer:

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:58.804
women with zero history of

00:08:58.804 --> 00:09:00.640
mental illness or criminal activity

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.000
deciding to kill their babies, and if
they'd actually done their numbers

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.680
properly and factored in the base rate
then the likelihood of her being guilty

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.839
would have gone from being a near
certainty

00:09:09.839 --> 00:09:14.160
to somewhere around only 13%

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.440
but despite the fact that her entire
conviction was based upon

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:21.680
just one piece of evidence, that was in
actuality a pile of statistical horsepoo

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.399
it took three years to get sally's

00:09:24.399 --> 00:09:27.839
conviction overturned
and even more tragically, Sally never

00:09:27.839 --> 00:09:31.519
really recovered from her ordeal
and she ended up

00:09:31.519 --> 00:09:35.040
passing away a few years later.
So yeah, sorry for the depressing story

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.440
but it really showcases why we need to
understand these kinds of

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:43.040
fallacies and concepts because COVID is
forcing us to

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.200
risk assess our own health right now, and
beyond that

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.120
we might end up on a jury where we
literally hold someone else's lives in

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.000
our hands.
So it's really important we know how to

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.399
we learn how to evaluate evidence
properly

00:09:56.399 --> 00:09:59.839
and more generally in this age of mass
information, with all these sort of

00:09:59.839 --> 00:10:02.800
conflicting theories
about the world going around,

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.720
it's helpful to find a sort of
quantified approach to truth-seeking

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.800
finding a balance that keeps both
our false positives and false negatives

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.640
as low as possible.
We don't want to be an overly

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:18.160
skeptical lion, dismissing
any new unconventional theory we read

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.120
but at the same time,
we don't want to be a gullible mouse

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:25.600
either, believing
any and every scary new or weird

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.040
idea or theory the internet comes up
with.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:32.160
so on that note thank you so much for
watching, and make sure to

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:35.760
subscribe to my channel because i'm
going to be making more videos on these

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.560
kind of sort of counter-intuitive
statistical topics,

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.079
and of course please like and share this
video if you found it helpful because,

00:10:42.079 --> 00:10:44.240
well,
you know how the internet works... if you

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.440
do that then more people see it and
i'd love that.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:53.200
So thank you &amp; see you next time!
